caddy-tui (1.10.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix: adding (or editing) a service on its own port (domain:8443) left the
    port unpublished in the docker compose, so the site was not reachable.
    After saving, caddy-tui now checks the compose file, offers to add the
    missing "ports" entries (validated, with backup) and to recreate the
    container. The diagnostics also flag Caddyfile ports missing from the
    compose file even when the running container still publishes them, and
    offer the same correction.

 -- Miguel González Oreiro <mgoreiro@gmail.com>  Wed, 28 Oct 2026 10:00:00 +0100

caddy-tui (1.10.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New diagnostics for plain HTTP served where HTTPS is expected (the
    browser error "SSL received a record that exceeded the maximum length"):
    an upstream whose scheme in the Caddyfile does not match what it speaks
    (http vs https, both end in a 502); a container that Caddy serves over
    HTTPS but that is also published in plain HTTP on the host; and a site
    whose port answers plain HTTP instead of TLS.

 -- Miguel González Oreiro <mgoreiro@gmail.com>  Mon, 26 Oct 2026 10:00:00 +0200

caddy-tui (1.9.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix: on Docker Desktop (macOS, and WSL) Docker reports the source of a
    bind mount with the path of its VM (/host_mnt/Users/me/Caddyfile), so the
    Caddyfile of a container created by docker compose was "not found". The
    path is now mapped back to the one on this machine.

 -- Miguel González Oreiro <mgoreiro@gmail.com>  Tue, 20 Oct 2026 11:00:00 +0200

caddy-tui (1.9.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix: every periodic check (and every validation) created and destroyed a
    throw-away container (docker run --rm caddy validate), which made Docker
    emit create/attach/start/die/destroy events that tools such as
    docker-controller-bot reported. With Caddy running, the configuration is
    now validated inside it (docker exec): the file it serves is validated as
    is, a candidate goes through stdin. A throw-away container is only used
    when Caddy is not running, when the text uses variables the container does
    not have, or when a candidate imports files by relative path.

 -- Miguel González Oreiro <mgoreiro@gmail.com>  Tue, 20 Oct 2026 09:00:00 +0200

caddy-tui (1.9.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Traffic statistics: [w] and --stats [24h|7d|30d|90d] show requests, traffic,
    latency, status classes, busiest hour, per hour/day bars, per-site lines and
    busiest client addresses over time. --collect-stats folds the access log
    since the last run into a SQLite database (hourly totals per site kept 90
    days, daily counts per address 30 days, mode 600); the screen collects
    first and --setup-alerts can run the collection on every check.
    Works per container and with --all.

 -- Miguel González Oreiro <mgoreiro@gmail.com>  Mon, 19 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0200

caddy-tui (1.8.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Several Caddy containers on one machine: at startup the last one used is
    the default choice, [S] switches to another one inside the program (the
    header shows "2/3 Caddy containers"), --list shows them all, and --check,
    --notify and --auto-block accept --all (grouped output, worst exit code,
    JSON with a "containers" list, Telegram messages labelled host/container).
    --setup-alerts asks whether to monitor all of them.
  * Alert state and scanner bans are now kept per container (the old shared
    files are read as a starting point).

 -- Miguel González Oreiro <mgoreiro@gmail.com>  Mon, 19 Oct 2026 09:00:00 +0200

caddy-tui (1.7.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Docker installed but unusable is now told apart from "no Caddy found":
    without permission it says to use sudo (or the docker group); with the
    service stopped it offers to start it (as root with systemd) or shows the
    command. In --check / --auto-block it exits with 3 and the same message.
    Before, both cases looked like "No Caddy container found" and offered to
    install a new Caddy.

