caddy-tui (1.6.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New [u]: undo the last change. Shows the diff against the latest earlier
    version of the Caddyfile and restores it after confirmation; the current
    version is backed up first, so [u] again redoes it.
  * Restore [b]: the list shows the age of each backup and how many lines
    would change, and the diff is shown before confirming. Restoring now
    offers to reload Caddy, and docker-compose backups no longer appear in
    the list.
  * The menu uses three columns on wide terminals.
  * New [p]: probe services. Requests every named site and reports HTTP
    status, response time and certificate validity, locally and through the
    public route. The diagnostics (so --check and the alerts) also check that
    every site answers, so a 5xx or an expired certificate raises an alert.
  * New [i]: blocked IPs. Block or unblock an IP or CIDR range on every site
    (a snippet defined before the first site plus an import in each one);
    [b] in the traffic monitor blocks one of the busiest addresses. New or
    edited sites keep the protection; the diagnostics warn if a site lacks it.
  * New [y]: update the Caddy image. Compares the running image digest with
    the registry (anonymous bearer token; Docker Hub, GHCR, Quay...), pulls
    the new one, validates the Caddyfile with it and only then recreates the
    container. The diagnostics warn when an update is available (cached 6 h).
  * New: caddy-tui --install (also offered when no Caddy container is found):
    guided setup of Caddy in Docker. Installs Docker on apt-based systems when
    missing, checks the machine (native Caddy, ports 80/443, DNS), writes a
    Caddyfile and docker-compose.yml, validates, starts Caddy and opens the
    interface. Closes the "install from scratch" request.
  * New: scanner detection and timed bans. [i] > "Review suspicious
    addresses" and the [b] list of the traffic monitor point out addresses that
    probe typical scanner paths, fail almost every request or fail many logins,
    and ban them for 24 h / 7 days / permanently; caddy-tui --auto-block does it
    unattended (also from --setup-alerts), lifting expired bans.
  * The add-service wizard now offers site templates: reverse proxy (as
    before), reverse proxy with path routing (/api to one app, /static to
    another, the rest to a default), redirect (www to apex, permanent or
    temporary, keeping path and query) and a maintenance page. The manage
    screen recognises and edits all of them with the same forms.

 -- Miguel González Oreiro <mgoreiro@gmail.com>  Sun, 18 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0200

caddy-tui (1.5.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New [m] screen: manage services. Lists the sites of the Caddyfile and
    edits them (with the wizard form when the block is one it could have
    generated; otherwise only that block in the editor) or removes them.
    Always validated in the context of the whole file, with a backup.
  * Comment lines containing braces no longer confuse the site detection.

 -- Miguel González Oreiro <mgoreiro@gmail.com>  Sun, 18 Oct 2026 09:00:00 +0200

caddy-tui (1.4.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New: caddy-tui --setup-alerts, a guided setup for Telegram alerts: asks
    for the bot token, finds the chat id, and installs a systemd service and
    timer (or a cron job) running caddy-tui --check --quiet --notify.
    --remove-alerts undoes it.
  * New: --notify sends a Telegram message once per problem (again if it
    changes or after 24 hours) and a notice when Caddy recovers.
  * Removed the static example files: the setup replaces them.

 -- Miguel González Oreiro <mgoreiro@gmail.com>  Sun, 18 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0200

caddy-tui (1.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New headless mode: caddy-tui --check [--quiet] [--json] runs the
    diagnostics once and exits with 0 (ok), 1 (warnings), 2 (problems) or 3
    (could not run), for cron, systemd timers and monitoring.
  * Examples installed: systemd service and timer, Telegram alert script.
  * Diagnostic hints no longer say "offered below" (they are also used
    headless); a fresh Caddy without certificates no longer triggers a warning.

 -- Miguel González Oreiro <mgoreiro@gmail.com>  Sat, 17 Oct 2026 23:00:00 +0200

caddy-tui (1.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New [h] screen: HTTPS certificates. Lists the certificates Caddy has
    stored (names, issuer, expiry) and warns when one is about to expire, when
    a domain has none yet, or when Let's Encrypt/ACME is failing for it.
  * The diagnostics also report certificate problems.

